.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt NQDCHUNK 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqdchunk ,
.Nm nqdpiece ,
.Nm nqdpieceat
.Nd digests of the chunks of a large file, and a piece checked alone
.Sh LIBRARY
libnqd, \-lnqd \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In nqd.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqdchunk
.Fa "const char *dir" "const char *path" "uint64_t chunk"
.Fa "char *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen" "char *why" "size_t wcap"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqdpiece
.Fa "const char *elem" "size_t n" "const char *piece" "uint64_t off"
.Fa "nqdrep **rep"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqdpieceat
.Fa "const char *elem" "size_t n" "const char *dir" "const char *path"
.Fa "nqdrep **rep"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqdchunk
reads the file
.Fa path
in
.Fa dir
once and writes to
.Fa out
a
.Ql chunks
element
.Pq Xr noqtis-data 5
in canonical JSON, for
.Xr nqdput 3 :
its path, its size, the chunk size
.Fa chunk
(1024 to 2^30 bytes) and the SHA-256 of each run of
.Fa chunk
bytes, the last run short.
The file is not split.
In symbols, with F the file's bytes, s its size, c the chunk size,
F[a:b] bytes a to b - 1, and HEX lower-case hex:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
d_i  = HEX(SHA-256(F[i c : min((i + 1) c, s)]))   i = 0 .. ceil(s/c) - 1
elem = {"chunk": c, "digests": [d_0, d_1, ...], "path": path, "size": s}
.Ed
.Pp
in canonical form; an empty file has no digest.
.Fa out
and
.Fa outlen
are as for
.Xr nqdjson 3 .
.Pp
.Fn nqdpiece
checks the file
.Fa piece ,
which holds the bytes of the element's file from byte
.Fa off
on (a multiple of the chunk size, at most the size), against the
.Ql chunks
element
.Fa elem
.Pf ( Fa n
bytes): each whole chunk in it, and a short last one when the piece ends
where the file does.
Only the piece is read.
The report's
.Ql chunk
rows name each chunk that differs, with its bytes (20 at most, then a
count), or say which chunks and bytes match; a short run at the end of a
piece that is not the end of the file is not checked, and a row says so.
.Pp
.Fn nqdpieceat
checks the whole file
.Fa path
in
.Fa dir
.Pf ( Ql /
between folders) the same way, from byte 0, opened as
.Fn nqdchunk
opens it: no folder on the way and not the file itself is followed when
it is a symbolic link (on Windows, a reparse point), and a link or
anything but a plain file is a failed row saying it is refused.
A file shorter than the recorded size fails.
Use it for a file of a capsule, and
.Fn nqdpiece
for a piece the caller names.
.Pp
A capsule's seal covers the digests through its data record, so
.Xr nqchkx 3
with
.Fa only
set to
.Pa noqtis.data.json
checks them without the file.
.Xr nqdchk 3
checks each recorded file whole against its chunks.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nqdchunk :
.Dv NQOK ;
.Dv NQEARG
for a chunk size out of bounds or a path a capsule cannot hold;
.Dv NQEIO
when the file cannot be read;
.Dv NQELIM
when the digests would not fit in a record (64 MiB: take larger chunks);
.Dv NQEBUF
for a
.Fa cap
too small;
.Dv NQEMEM
when memory runs out.
A link or anything but a plain file is
.Dv NQEARG .
.Pp
.Fn nqdpiece
and
.Fn nqdpieceat :
.Dv NQOK
when every chunk read matches and there was at least one (or the file is
empty);
.Dv NQEVRFY
otherwise, also for an element that is not valid;
.Fa *rep
is set in both cases
.Pq Xr nqdchk 3 .
.Dv NQEARG
for a
.Dv NULL
argument.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Record the 1 MiB chunks of a file and check a piece fetched from byte
2 MiB on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static char el[1 << 16];
char why[256];
size_t n;
nqdrep *rep = NULL;
int rc = nqdchunk("results", "data/big.bin", 1 << 20, el, sizeof el,
                  &n, why, sizeof why);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqdpiece(el, n, "/tmp/piece.bin", (uint64_t)2 << 20, &rep);
nqdrepfree(rep);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-chunk 1 ,
.Xr nqdchk 3 ,
.Xr nqdnew 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-data 5
